
.TH TTY 1 "October 2026" "tty" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tty \- print the terminal name connected to standard input
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tty
.RB [ \-s ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tty
prints the filename of the terminal connected to standard input.
.PP
If standard input is associated with a terminal, its pathname
is printed, typically in the form
.B /dev/tty
or
.BR /dev/pts/N .
.PP
If standard input is not associated with a terminal,
.B tty
prints
.B not a tty
and exits with status 1.
.PP
With the
.B \-s
option, no output is produced.
The exit status alone indicates whether standard input
is connected to a terminal.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-silent ", " \-\-quiet
Print nothing, returning only an exit status indicating
whether standard input is a terminal.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display usage information and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
Display the terminal connected to standard input:
.PP
.RS
.B tty
.RE
.PP
Example output:
.PP
.RS
.B /dev/pts/0
.RE
.PP
Test whether standard input is connected to a terminal:
.PP
.RS
.nf
if tty -s; then
    echo "Running in a terminal"
else
    echo "Not running in a terminal"
fi
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check the terminal status without printing anything:
.PP
.RS
.B tty --quiet
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Standard input is connected to a terminal.
.TP
.B 1
Standard input is not connected to a terminal.
.TP
.B 2
An invalid option or unexpected argument was specified.
.TP
.B 3
An output error occurred.
.SH STANDARDS
The basic functionality conforms to POSIX
.BR tty .
The
.BR \-\-silent ,
.BR \-\-quiet ,
and
.B \-\-help
options are GNU extensions.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR stty (1),
.BR isatty (3),
.BR ttyname (3),
.BR tty (4)
